  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Palauans\"\nsubtitle: \"Palau\"\nregion: \"Oceania\"\ntags: [ethnography, oceania]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | Palauans |\n| Where | Palau, Oceania |\n| How many | unknown |\n| Language | Palauan |\n| Religion | unknown |\n| Known for | carved storyboards · turtle-shell vessels · plaited fibre costumes · war canoes with pearlshell inlay · taro feasts |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Palauans are an oceanic people whose material culture is documented primarily through British Museum holdings. Their objects reveal a maritime society that works with reef materials — shark teeth, swordfish jaw, turtle shell, and pearlshell — shaped into weapons, vessels, and ceremonial items. Taro feasts, carved narrative boards, and gendered dance traditions emerge as central to their cultural life.\n\n## Material culture\n\nPalauan material culture draws entirely from the reef, shore and forest — wood, turtle-shell, pearlshell, and plaited plant fibres, often combined with inlaid shell and red pigment.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nPalauan textiles use plant fibres — banana, hibiscus, and pandanus — either woven on looms or plaited by hand.\n\n- **Loom-woven bast cloth** — A loincloth woven from banana leaf fibre and hibiscus fibre on a loom.\n- **Dyed hibiscus-fibre dance dress** — A woman's dance skirt of hibiscus fibre dyed yellow with turmeric and red, hung from a waistband.\n- **Plaited leaf work** — Leaf strips plaited or woven into hat crowns, dance-brassieres, wrist-ornaments and baskets.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nDress is almost entirely ceremonial and mostly for women, made from dyed and plaited plant fibres with no woven cloth.\n\n- **Women's dance costume** — A complete ensemble of dyed hibiscus skirt, woven leaf-strip brassiere, vegetable-fibre belt, and plaited leaf wrist-ornaments.\n- **Men's loincloth** — A loom-woven garment of banana and hibiscus fibre, shown in red on carved storyboards.\n- **Plaited leaf hat** — A hat with a crown of plaited leaf strips, the only recorded head covering.\n\n### Architecture\n\nThe *bai* is the recognisable traditional house, appearing on storyboards and lending its form to carved ceremonial objects.\n\n- **Bai** — The traditional Palauan house, shown on storyboards and used as the shape for a carved ceremonial board.\n- **Waterside platforms** — Platforms at the water's edge shown on storyboards alongside houses and trees.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nPalauan makers work in wood, turtle-shell and shell inlay, with no metalwork recorded and only one ceramic piece.\n\n- **Turtle-shell vessels** — Polished plates, dishes, bowls, spoons and fish-hooks carved from turtle-shell, some with lugs or pointed ends.\n- **Shell-inlaid containers** — Wooden vessels and bowls inlaid with white clam shell and pearlshell, often coloured with red pigment.\n- **Taro feast table** — A red six-legged wooden table inlaid with white clam shell and pearlshell, made to display taro at feasts.\n- **Taro pounder** — A carved and polished hardwood tool for processing taro.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nPersonal ornament uses reef materials — carved turtle-shell, sea urchin spines, and shell beads — along with tattooing.\n\n- **Turtle-shell bangle** — A carved and polished bracelet made from turtle-shell.\n- **Sea urchin spine necklace** — Nine brown-and-white striped sea urchin spines pierced and strung on twisted fibre cords.\n- **Money-pendant** — A single reddish-brown bead hung on cord, used as both currency and ornament.\n- **Tattooing** — A practice attested by a tattooing-instrument on a wooden haft and a storyboard depicting the process.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nNo instruments, song genres or musical occasions are named in the sources, though dance had purpose-made equipment.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nDance is sharply gendered, with men using display weapons and women wearing elaborate plaited-fibre costumes.\n\n- **Dance-spear** — A wooden spear with a flattened barbed head and shaft painted with black crosses and red spots.\n- **Dance-sword** — A wooden sword tapering to a point, painted in black, yellow and red across both faces of the blade.\n- **Dance-sticks** — Paired wooden sticks with leaf strips attached near one end.\n- **Women's dance costume** — A complete ensemble of dyed skirt, woven brassiere, belt and wrist-ornaments of plaited plant fibres.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nFeasting and ritual are documented through objects, with taro displays and ceremonial swords marking public and ritual occasions.\n\n- **Taro feast** — A public celebration centred on displaying taro on an inlaid feast table.\n- **Brotech** — A ceremonial sword of wood inlaid with white and cream-coloured shell, classified as religious or ritual equipment.\n\n## Foodways\n\nTaro is the named staple, celebrated in feast tables and pounder tools; fishing and turtling are evident from material culture.\n\n- **Taro** — The staple crop processed with a wooden pounder, displayed on feast tables and shown in storyboard scenes.\n- **Fishing and turtling** — Subsistence practices evident from turtle-shell vessels, fish-hooks and representations of boats with catch on storyboards.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nNarrative is carried on carved storyboards depicting origin stories, historical battles, currency and social practices.\n\n- **Llochukl el chesbocheb** — A carved storyboard shaped as a *bai* that depicts the discovery of turtles' egg-laying and natural childbirth on its two faces, with the 'lady of lost virtue' at each end.\n- **Battle storyboard** — A carved board depicting a historical fight between canoes, with a central warrior surrounded by red-hulled vessels of spear-wielders, shore buildings and trees.\n- **Currency storyboard** — A carved board showing a boat with men and women in conversation before three large stone discs of currency and a background of buildings and trees.\n- **Tattooing storyboard** — A carved board depicting male and female figures engaged in tattooing against trees and a *bai*.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nThree Palauan words are recorded — *bai*, *brotech* and *llochukl el chesbocheb* — but no information on language family or religion appears in the sources.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *bai* — A traditional Palauan house.\n- *brotech* — A ceremonial sword of wood inlaid with shell, used in ritual.\n- *llochukl el chesbocheb* — A carved storyboard shaped as a house, depicting origin narratives.\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- British Museum online collection, Palau material — the catalogue records of the 43 objects shown here, covering weapons, dance equipment, turtle-shell vessels, plaited fibre costume, the taro feast table, the war canoe and five carved storyboards: https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection\n- No Wikipedia article and no UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage inscription were available for this profile; the general article, not used as a source above, is at https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palauans\n- Smithsonian Folkways search for Palauan and Palauan-adjacent recordings, offered as a starting point rather than as a source used here: https://folkways.si.edu/search?query=Palau\n\n",
